Nice of you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.480
check out this video on the AI Coffee Break&nbsp;
channel, that just surpassed 500 subscribers!&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.920
[party sounds]
Crazy!&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.840
Ms. Coffee Bean and me still cannot believe&nbsp;
it! This channel, that started first as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.000
creative and playful spin-off to my&nbsp;
semester of teaching in Corona mode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.360
now has so many subscribers, that&nbsp;
follow the content regularly!

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.880
This is 20 times as many people&nbsp;
than I have taught in one semester,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.080
and on YouTube we can explain things to&nbsp;
the whole globe and do this even when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.080
I am sleeping! because Ms. Coffee&nbsp;
Bean does the whole explaining.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.440
Thank you all for joining&nbsp;
us in our humble beginnings.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.880
Ms. Coffee Bean will do her best&nbsp;
not to disappoint you in the future!

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.440
Ok, enough of this, now we get&nbsp;
to the topic of today’s video:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.560
This video is taking a breathing pause,
[breathing in and out]&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:05.040
after Ms. Coffee Bean is quite overwhelmed with&nbsp;
recent advances like GPT-3 in Machine Learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.560
especially in natural language&nbsp;
processing or short NLP.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.760
So if you feel a little overwhelmed too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.360
let’s take a top-down theoretical view on the&nbsp;
whole situation of data-driven language models.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.640
In this video, we will address 3&nbsp;
important questions: What is meaning?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:29.760
How far is state-of-the-art in NLP from meaning?
And: Do publications misuse the word meaning?

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.160
Especially, we are discussing the paper&nbsp;
of Emily Bender and Alexander Koller.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.400
This is not only Ms. Coffee Beans&nbsp;
favorite paper of the month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.960
but it won the best theme paper award at ACL 2020&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.840
for the theme of the conference "Taking Stock&nbsp;
of Where We’ve Been and Where We’re Going".

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.880
Even though this video is about NLP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.280
we think there are important messages&nbsp;
that everyone in AI should think about.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.800
The paper delivers theoretical considerations&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.480
about what a language model trained purely&nbsp;
on text completion can learn about meaning.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.000
CB: What is meaning?

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.800
Well, wait for it, we will come to that later.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:17.040
Now we ask: How often have you heard academic&nbsp;
publications, news or even Ms. Coffee Bean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.720
say that large language models, especially&nbsp;
Transformer architectures like GPT-3 or BERT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.760
“understand” natural language and learn “meaning”?

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.800
Far too often, to be honest.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.680
And in Ms. Coffee Beans defense, she&nbsp;
also puts it like this from time to time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.960
trying to simplify things&nbsp;
and deliver messages quickly.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.600
Otherwise, she is afraid she might&nbsp;
be spending too much time on defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.960
appropriate terms or opening a&nbsp;
philosophical discussion about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.640
how far we are on the ladder towards&nbsp;
natural language “understanding”.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.400
But now there is no more way around this.&nbsp;
It is time to discuss this with the help of&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.720
this paper and at least warn that words&nbsp;
like “meaning” and “understanding” can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:06.880
very easily misused and misinterpreted when we are&nbsp;
talking about language models like BERT or GPT-x.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.920
And this leads to an undesirable mismatch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.200
between what research tells it&nbsp;
does and what it really does.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.240
So the authors write: “We argue&nbsp;
that the language modelling task,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.560
because it only uses form as training data,&nbsp;
cannot in principle lead to learning of meaning.”

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.320
This is a very strong claim to accept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.880
especially if you have seen BERT&nbsp;
perform magic on biology data,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:40.080
or seen GPT-3 translate, make copycat&nbsp;
analogies and many other things.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:47.040
But wait a little, what does this claim&nbsp;
even mean? We will not be on the same page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:53.280
until we have explained the terminology.&nbsp;
So it is time for the “terminology taser!”

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.200
It is very important to define what&nbsp;
a language model is. With “language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:05.760
model” the authors (quote) “refer to any system&nbsp;
trained only on the task of string prediction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:12.320
whether it operates over characters, words or&nbsp;
sentences, and sequentially or not.” (end quote)

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.600
So BERT, predicting masked&nbsp;
words or tokens in sentences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.360
is definitely falling into the&nbsp;
category of a language model.&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.520
Now: What does meaning mean? Quote:&nbsp;
“We take (linguistic) meaning to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:32.320
the relation between a linguistic form&nbsp;
and communicative intent.” [buzz disk]

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.200
Wait, what? Have we just explained&nbsp;
a term with two other ones? Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.920
but don’t worry, because we are&nbsp;
at the terminology bottom here!&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.360
To get away from the range of the terminology&nbsp;
taser, we will now build an example based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:54.160
Alice and Bob talking to each other: Alice is&nbsp;
hungry and she wants Bob to bring her food.&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.200
That is her communicative intent,&nbsp;
she wants him to get her food.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.680
But for Bob to get the message, Alice needs&nbsp;
to realize her communicative intent into&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:13.760
a form, or into something in the real world.&nbsp;
This could be imperative air vibrations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:21.120
Bobs ears perceive as Alice’s commanding voice&nbsp;
“get me food!”, the trace of a pen on paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:27.600
the pixels of this video, or the hands and the&nbsp;
face in signed languages. Now, for Bob to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.840
the message, he has to understand the meaning,
so he has to find out the right combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.240
all possible pairs of expressions&nbsp;
and intents that Alice could have.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.800
Notice that Bob does not have access to the&nbsp;
communicative intent, but only to the form.&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.920
Then, if he gets the right meaning&nbsp;
of the form without access to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.760
communicative intent, then he has&nbsp;
an understanding of the situation.&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:00.400
But he has to be careful, because the&nbsp;
meaning of Alice’s “Get me food” expression,

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.920
is not to be confused with&nbsp;
the many conventional meanings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.600
to Alice’s expression: For example, she might&nbsp;
have said “Get me food” as a request for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:15.040
something to eat, or she might just wanted&nbsp;
Bob to bring her their dog called “Food”.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.240
“This is not a conventional meaning of&nbsp;
Food”, you might say. Well, keep in mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:25.040
that this is only the term for describing&nbsp;
all potential meanings of an expression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:30.000
The conventional meaning does not have to be&nbsp;
neither widely accepted, nor in any dictionary:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.240
Think about you and your best friends&nbsp;
inventing words or using words as a code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.640
That meaning is in no dictionary, but&nbsp;
you both know what that utterance means.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:44.560
But just imagine: The gap between form&nbsp;
and meaning is bigger than one thinks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.800
because the number of communicative intents&nbsp;
is huge. We as humans, cheat a little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.920
because we have access to the physical, social&nbsp;
or mental situation of our interlocutor.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.680
But a language model, so a system that was&nbsp;
trained only on strings to do string completion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:07.600
does not. And while humans given a form,&nbsp;
try really hard to make a mental model of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.360
the communicative intent of the interlocutor&nbsp;
and understand the meaning, a language model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.280
never does: It gets the form and computes&nbsp;
similarities and likelihoods to other forms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:23.920
that have to be the right response, without&nbsp;
building a model of the communicative intent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.800
and this one is crucial in the&nbsp;
relation between form and meaning.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.200
So the authors argue, that quote:&nbsp;
“the language modelling task,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:38.800
because it only uses form as training data,&nbsp;
cannot in principle lead to learning of meaning.”

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:44.720
Yes, so what? GPT-3 is still performing&nbsp;
like it would know a lot about the world!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:51.680
Well, it seems like quote “their apparent&nbsp;
ability to “reason” is sometimes a mirage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:58.480
built on leveraging artifacts in the training&nbsp;
data (i.e. form, not meaning).” Endquote.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:03.200
All that a language model has seen, is an&nbsp;
enormous amount of utterances about the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.720
but it does not see clues for the communicative&nbsp;
intent and it also doesn’t try to model it.&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.760
To put it another way: would you expect a&nbsp;
child to learn what language means , if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.560
did not interact with the world at all, but hear&nbsp;
a recorded tape and trying to repeat the words?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:25.040
To make this even more clear,
the authors propose an Octopus thought experiment.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.920
Ms. Coffee Bean retells it now with minor&nbsp;
alterations: Once upon a later time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:37.120
we have again Alice and Bob, now separated on two&nbsp;
different islands, because Bob did not succeed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.640
bringing Alice food. But because they still miss&nbsp;
each other, they are sending messages to each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:50.720
other through an underwater cable.
Only that Mr. O, this octopus here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:54.240
is super intelligent and has tapped&nbsp;
into their communication line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:00.800
Of course, Mr. O does not speak English, but&nbsp;
over time he learns patterns and can predict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:07.360
what Bob’s responses to Alice are and starts&nbsp;
sending messages to Alice instead of Bob.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:13.120
Alice does not notice the exchange, since Mr.&nbsp;
O is getting really good at this impersonation.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:18.560
He can even realize that some words are used in&nbsp;
similar contexts and can be exchanged if needed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:24.480
can construct whole plausible sentences. Only that&nbsp;
Mr. O has never seen the objects that Alice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:30.000
Bob are talking about. Because their communication&nbsp;
is kind of regular, this is enough for the while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.080
when not much changes on Alice’s and Bob’s&nbsp;
islands. But one day, Alice says that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:42.480
invented a coconut catapult and sends the building&nbsp;
plan to Bob. Mr. O intercepts the message, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:47.760
can not rebuild the invention, because he&nbsp;
does not know what ropes or coconuts are.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:54.000
But from earlier conversations, he noticed&nbsp;
that Bob would react very excited like this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.800
“Great job!” But did he really understand what&nbsp;
the coconut catapult is and what it is for?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:03.040
No, he did not grasp the&nbsp;
communicative intent of Alice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:06.400
because he misses important&nbsp;
observations about her world.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:14.240
Happy with Mr. O’s reply impersonating Bob, Alice&nbsp;
asks: “What should I use the coconut catapult for?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.600
Should I use it for sending&nbsp;
you coconuts to your islands or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:21.440
should I use it for defending against&nbsp;
the bear lurking in the bush?”

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:29.600
Well, Mr. O has a problem now, not knowing how to&nbsp;
respond. What is the friction coefficient of air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:35.280
how does gravity without the Archimedes&nbsp;
force works? How far are the islands apart?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:40.560
How much does Bob want a coconut? He would&nbsp;
not even get this far with these questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.800
because what is a coconut and what&nbsp;
is a catapult in the first place?

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:51.200
All he knows, is that coconuts are used in the&nbsp;
context of food and catapults in the context of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:57.440
defending. But can coconuts defend against a&nbsp;
bear? Can coconuts fly from Island to island?

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:02.880
Mr. O had a great time impersonating Bob, but&nbsp;
now he has to admit his defeat. We hope that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:08.320
allegory of Mr. O was meant to clarify that&nbsp;
language models are in Mr. O’s situation.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:15.360
So what to do? We have to keep in mind, that all&nbsp;
arguments so far hold only for language models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:19.520
so systems that are trained to&nbsp;
predict missing strings in a sequence.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:25.360
We can also think about new tasks and&nbsp;
datasets that are augmented with meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:29.680
for example about those that try to&nbsp;
ground language in a visual situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.400
For more on this, check out our video linked&nbsp;
right now and also in the description below.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:41.280
Also, that a language model does not acquire&nbsp;
meaning, does not mean that BERT does not deliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:47.440
amazing results. This paper tries to set a&nbsp;
warning sign that reveals what meaning is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:54.080
how far state-of-the-art systems are from meaning&nbsp;
and how publications misuse the word meaning.

00:11:54.080 --> 00:12:00.240
In a field where after GPT-2 comes&nbsp;
GPT-3 with even more impressive results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.720
by only being trained on language&nbsp;
modelling, we have to take a break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:11.120
to think about what the system let’s us&nbsp;
think it can do and what it can really do.

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:16.800
Because we did not address all aspect of the&nbsp;
paper, Ms. Coffee Bean really recommends you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:21.120
read the paper yourself or even have&nbsp;
the authors read it out loud for you!&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:23.840
Links to the paper and to Soundcloud&nbsp;
are in the description below.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:28.880
Let us know in the comments what you think!
This is kind of a controversial and upsetting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:34.320
topic and it might need some time for digestion.
Thanks for watching patiently until the end!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:37.840
See you next time!
Bye!

